    <h1>Impacts and what we can do</h1> 
</header> 

<main> 
      
      <section class="impacts-grid"> 

        <div class="impacts-box global">
            <span>Global Impacts</span> 

            <p>Problematic social media use can negatively affect young people's mental health, 
            sleep, education and relationships around the world. According to the World Health 
            Organization (WHO), a 2022 international survey of almost 280,000 young people across 
            44 countries and regions found that 11% showed signs of problematic social media use, 
            increasing from 7% in 2018. Problematic use can involve struggling to control time 
            online and neglecting everyday responsibilities. In New Zealand, a 2025 survey of 
            540 teenagers aged 13–17 found that 22% met the criteria for problematic social media 
            use, while 49% said social media disrupted activities such as homework, chores, family 
            time and sleep. These impacts can affect not only teenagers but also their families, schools 
            and wider communities. However, these surveys measure different populations and use different 
            methods, so their percentages should not be directly compared as if they were the same study.</p> 
        </div> 

        <div class="impacts-box NZ"> 
            <span>Image</span> 
        </div> 

        <div class="impacts-box positive">
            <span>Positive Impacts</span> 

            <p>Although problematic use can cause harm, social media can also have positive 
            impacts when used responsibly. It allows young people to communicate with friends 
            and family, access educational resources, express their creativity and connect with 
            people who share their interests. It can be especially valuable for marginalised young 
            people who may feel isolated in their offline communities, as online spaces can help them 
            find understanding, encouragement and a sense of belonging. The WHO reported that some 
            adolescents who used social media frequently without showing problematic behaviours also 
            experienced stronger peer support and social connections. UNICEF's 2025 report, Childhood 
            in a Digital World, also explains that digital technology can help children develop skills 
            and explore opportunities online. This shows that social media itself is not always harmful; 
            its effects depend on factors such as how it is used, the content encountered and whether young 
            people feel safe and supported online.</p>
        </div> 

        <div class="impacts-box we"> 
            <span>What we can do</span> 

            <p>To reduce problematic social media use in New Zealand, teenagers can set screen-time 
            limits and balance social media with schoolwork, exercise and face-to-face interactions. 
            Schools should teach digital literacy and online safety, while parents encourage healthy 
            habits. Technology companies should make their platforms safer, and the government should 
            introduce appropriate protections for young users. According to 2025 research, 47% of surveyed 
            NZ teenagers and 77% of caregivers supported raising the minimum social media access age to 16. 
            In my opinion, combining education, safer platform designs and age-appropriate protections is 
            the best way to reduce harm while keeping the positive benefits of social media.</p>
        </div> 

        <div class="impacts-box schools"> 
            <span>What schools can do</span> 

            <p>Schools can help reduce problematic social media use by teaching students about 
            digital literacy, online safety, cyberbullying and the effects of excessive screen time 
            on mental health, sleep and learning. Teachers could run workshops to help students 
            understand how social media algorithms influence their behaviour and how to recognise 
            harmful content. Schools should also encourage students to take regular breaks from 
            devices, participate in sports and other activities, and build stronger face-to-face 
            relationships. Providing counselling and support for students experiencing cyberbullying 
            or online stress is also important. These actions can help teenagers develop healthier 
            online habits and use social media more responsibly.</p>
        </div> 

